   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "A couple months ago someone asked about putting regression equations\r<br>in each facet:\r<br>\r<br><a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/topic/ggplot2/me5hL8XOxTo/discussion\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/topic/ggplot2/me5hL8XOxTo/discussion&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792375083520000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3OOQctb5ABCDzgdh-D7g_A\">https://groups.google.com/d/topic/ggplot2/me5hL8XOxTo/discussion</a>\r<br>\r<br>Your question is a variant on this, so read the above link. Basically\r<br>you want to write a short function that will compute and return the\r<br>correlation (or whatever you want) for a subset of data, use ddply on\r<br>your data to generate a new data frame, and then annotate the facets\r<br>with geom_text.\r<br>\r<br>Ben\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>On Mon, Jul 23, 2012 at 8:51 PM, hlm &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">hideyos...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hi guys,\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; This is a copy of a question I put up on stack overflow, as I didn&#39;t realise\r<br>&gt; there was a dedicated group for ggplot2 before I posted it, but thought I\r<br>&gt; should let you guys know.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Anyway here is the question and the link is below.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; When using a facet_grid in ggplot2 I would like to be able to have value of\r<br>&gt; the correlation for the subsetted data for each grid cell in the top right\r<br>&gt; corner of the specific plot.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; e.g. if running:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; p &lt;- ggplot(mtcars, aes(mpg, wt)) + geom_point()\r<br>&gt; p + facet_grid(vs ~ am, margins=TRUE)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; I would like to see the value for correlation for each of the 9 plots in the\r<br>&gt; grid somewhere. In this specific case from the example, I would expect each\r<br>&gt; to be close to -0.9 or so from visual inspection.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Or perhaps an output table to go with the plot that gives the correlation\r<br>&gt; values for each of the cells in the table matching up with the\r<br>&gt; facet_grid...(this is less desirable but also an option).\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Ideally I would like to extend this to any other function I choose that so\r<br>&gt; that it can use either or both of the two variables plotted to calculate\r<br>&gt; statistics.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Is this possible?\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Thanks in advance\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; <a href=\"http://stackoverflow.com/questions/11622526/correlation-values-in-a-facet-grid-from-ggplot2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://stackoverflow.com/questions/11622526/correlation-values-in-a-facet-grid-from-ggplot2&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792375083520000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2EV81doQaZqHLkpJEHRqmN\">http://stackoverflow.com/questions/11622526/correlation-values-in-a-facet-grid-from-ggplot2</a>\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; --\r<br>&gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the ggplot2 mailing\r<br>&gt; list.\r<br>&gt; Please provide a reproducible example:\r<br>&gt; <a href=\"https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792375083520000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3_SL6liz-6XDfkI10t_TFG\">https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility</a>\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; To post: email <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggp...@googlegroups.com</a>\r<br>&gt; To unsubscribe: email <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggplot2+u...@googlegroups.com</a>\r<br>&gt; More options: <a href=\"http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792375083520000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1IZMFC0GCIfDNOVfdZXNE_\">http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2</a>\r<br>"
